  "worked_solution": "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,1⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡\\alpha =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\alpha )}{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}]{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-(0-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -0-(0-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
